# What a build rooted here does not want in its context.
#
# The context is the repository root, so without this a build uploads the venv
# and every worktree — a gigabyte of things the image never opens.
# `offline/Dockerfile.dockerignore` does the same job for that build, and is named
# for its Dockerfile because that is how BuildKit scopes an ignore file to one
# build. **A build whose Dockerfile has one of those does not read this file at
# all**, so this covers only builds that have none.

# Virtual environments, whatever the project calls them.
.venv/
venv/
*-venv/

.git/
.claude/

# **Files that hold each developer's own values.** Both are gitignored, so
# neither is usually here — but a build context is not the place to find out.
# Mount them at run time instead, which is what keeps a credential out of an
# image layer.
.env
.env.*
environments.yaml

# **Every `.env`, wherever it sits, and that is what `**/` buys.** The rules
# above match only the context root, so a service's file would otherwise have to
# be listed by name, and the list goes stale the moment a service moves.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Nothing here is imported by the package.
tests/
docs/
offline/
tools/
.git-.git-hooks/
.git-hooks/
.github/
.scratch/

# Python's own leavings, which vary by machine and would bust the layer cache.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
.coverage
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
